% VERIFIED (here): the three quoted passages on pp. 45--47 are the ones this
%   deck's ltnotes rely on (contrast before generalisation; simultaneous
%   juxtaposition); re-read as recorded in the variation-theory skill, which
%   verified them against the book.
% CLAIM: Contrast must be experienced simultaneously (instances juxtaposed),
%   not reconstructed from memory, for the varying aspect to be discerned;
%   and generalisation before contrast is induction, which cannot open a
%   dimension the learner has never discerned.
% FOUND-VIA: inherited from modules/files/slides/ltnotes.bib (branch
%   notes-on-files) where it backs the same variation-pattern notes.
% PICKED: the primary book-length statement of variation theory's necessary
%   conditions, used course-wide in ltnotes.
% QUOTE (pp. 45--47): "if you cannot see the greenness of any of three green,
%   though otherwise different, things, you cannot see what they have in
%   common, either" (p. 46); of two teachers juxtaposing objects, "[o]ne is
%   betting on induction, the other on contrast. The latter wins" (p. 47);
%   discernment comes from "the simultaneous experience of things that differ
%   in color but are the same otherwise" (p. 46).
% VERIFIED: pp. 45--47 quotes as recorded in the variation-theory skill
%   (verified there against the book); Routledge lists the print year as
%   2014, the 2015 year is kept for key-consistency with the files deck.
% DATE: 2026-07-20 (there); reused here 2026-09-03
